1 NOTE Johan Ludvig Runeberg was fifth cousin, twice removed, to Walter Gentala Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHAN LUDVIG RUNEBERG    (By Lars Granholm - Runeberg was  my great-grandmother's 4th cousin) 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Ludvig Runeberg was born February 5, 1804 in Jakobstad, Finland. As a youngster he had seen some of the famous generals, both Finnish and Russian, after the 1808-09 war. As a student, he had a tutoring position in a manor ho
2 CONC use with an officer's family in the interior of Finland. There he also met a   soldier from that war and took notes of his tales. These were later published under the name "The Tales of Ensign Stål" in 1848. 
2 CONT Finland had been part of Sweden since 1157. The Swedish rule was beneficial on the whole, and the Finns were loyal to the common king. The Finns had regularly fought with the Swedes in the many wars against Russia. Sweden and Russ
2 CONC ia had been allies against Napoleon, but when Czar Alexander I went over to Napoleon's side and demanded that Sweden should join him, king Gustaf IV refused. Without even a declaration of war, the Czar attacked his recent ally, hi
2 CONC s troops entering Finland on February 21, 1808. 
2 CONT The Finns were quite unprepared and eventually were defeated after a bitter war. In March in 1809 Finland became an autonomous Grand Duchy of Russia, allowed to keep its present, Swedish laws. 
2 CONT Although the Finns enjoined considerable freedom initially, subsequent Russian Governors began to impose Russian influence on Finland. The Finns passively resisted this and one of the ways in which this was manifested was in the l
2 CONC iterature, music, and poesy. 
2 CONT Runeberg's poetry was one of the influences that awoke the yearning for independence which eventually was achieved in 1917. A similar influence came through music, notably, if later, by Sibelius' "Finlandia." Many of Runeberg's po
2 CONC ems have been set to music. He also wrote the words for Finland's national anthem, "Our Land", first sung in 1846. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The first poem in "The Tales of Ensign Stål" sets the tone for the subsequent ones: 
2 CONT
2 CONT      ENSIGN STÅL. 
2 CONT
2 CONT What joy it is, alone at night, 
2 CONT To feel my thoughts returning 
2 CONT To youthful years, wherein the light 
2 CONT Of friendly stars is burning! 
2 CONT Who'll join me on the road I take 
2 CONT To Näsijärvi's dusky lake? 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Twas there I learned to know a man,- 
2 CONT An ensign's rank he'd won him 
2 CONT In soldier days, but now the ban 
2 CONT Of ill luck was upon him. 
2 CONT He'd come to live, the Lord knows why, 
2 CONT At the same estate as I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My station left me naught at all 
2 CONT To wish, in my opinion. 
2 CONT I was a tutor at "the hall," 
2 CONT Was truly fortune's minion. 
2 CONT Of great folks' fare I freely fed; 
2 CONT The old man lived on parish bread. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I smoked a mixture with a crest, 
2 CONT A meerschaum pipe I flaunted; 
2 CONT Old Stål had only plug at best, 
2 CONT And that supply was scanted, 
2 CONT So that quite often, at a loss, 
2 CONT He'd stuff his birchen pipe with moss. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Oh age of gold, Oh life that gleams 
2 CONT With purest joy and pleasure, 
2 CONT When one is young, and student dreams 
2 CONT Fill every rift of leisure, 
2 CONT And one's worst worry is the fear 
2 CONT That one's mustache will not appear! 
2 CONT
2 CONT Self-centered in my happy lot, 
2 CONT I little knew life's rigor. 
2 CONT My arm was strong, my blood ran hot, 
2 CONT My cheek was bright and vigor. 
2 CONT So brimful I of zest and youth, 
2 CONT No king could be more proud, forsooth! 
2 CONT
2 CONT But ensign Stål did not complain, 
2 CONT Though lonely and unheeded, 
2 CONT He puffed his pipe, he wove his seine, 
2 CONT While on we others speeded. 
2 CONT Good Lord! compared to such as he 
2 CONT How mightily important we! 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Twas fun to watch as in those days 
2 CONT He plied his occupation; 
2 CONT His wrinkled face, his awkward ways, 
2 CONT His coat of antique fashion; 
2 CONT His nose a jutting aquiline, 
2 CONT With glasses of absurd design. 
2 CONT
2 CONT How often by his cottage fire 
2 CONT I'd sit and play the devil! 
2 CONT Fine sport it was to fan his ire, 
2 CONT To make his nets unravel 
2 CONT By knotting all the loops awry. 
2 CONT Good glory! how the sparks would fly! 
2 CONT
2 CONT He'd jump up, yell "Be off with ye!" 
2 CONT But soon his wrath abated; 
2 CONT A pipeful, an apology, 
2 CONT And I'd be reinstated. 
2 CONT I'd saunter in next day, and then 
2 CONT The comedy'd begin again. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That he, like me, had had what youth 
2 CONT So lavishly provided, 
2 CONT And that he knew far more, in truth, 
2 CONT Of what life meant than I did, 
2 CONT All this I was by far too wise 
2 CONT In college lore to recognize. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I guessed not that with sword in hand 
2 CONT He once had strived and cheerily 
2 CONT Offered his heart's blood for the land 
2 CONT I since have loved so dearly. 
2 CONT My thoughts ran wild in everything; 
2 CONT He was but ensign, I a king. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then for some reason life went wrong; 
2 CONT My zest for it was finished. 
2 CONT The winter day was far too long, 
2 CONT Though daylight hours diminished. 
2 CONT All was so different from before, 
2 CONT Each minute seemed an endless bore. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I sought the bookshelf and took out 
2 CONT A volume just for pastime; 
2 CONT Anonymous it was, about 
2 CONT How Finland fought the last time. 
2 CONT It stood unbound and out of place 
2 CONT Amid the rest, as in disgrace.
2 CONT
2 CONT I bore this book off, opened it 
2 CONT And started idly skimming, 
2 CONT When suddenly I chanced to hit 
2 CONT What sent my senses swimming. 
2 CONT I read a sentence, I read two; 
2 CONT My heart began to throb anew. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Oh what a land, what men were these, 
2 CONT How resolute, how glorious! 
2 CONT An army that could starve and freeze 
2 CONT And yet remain victorious! 
2 CONT Onward and ever on I sped; 
2 CONT I could have kissed each page I read. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The hour of danger spurred this band 
2 CONT To bolder resolution. 
2 CONT What love didst thou inspire, Oh land, 
2 CONT Despite thy destitution,- 
2 CONT A love so strong, a love so sweet 
2 CONT From those thou gave'st but bark to eat! 
2 CONT
2 CONT A world now beckoned, which my thought 
2 CONT Had never yet laid eyes on, 
2 CONT My life expanded as it sought 
2 CONT This magic new horizon. 
2 CONT Too short the book, for now my day 
2 CONT Took wings and flew without a stay. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I put the finished volume by, 
2 CONT The evening hours had dwindled, 
2 CONT But none the less my hearth burned high 
2 CONT With flame the spark had kindled. 
2 CONT So much there was to ask about- 
2 CONT But maybe Stål could help me out. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I found him seated, much the same, 
2 CONT Above his fishnets leaning. 
2 CONT His eyes, that glanced up as I came, 
2 CONT Could have no other meaning 
2 CONT Than, "When shall I be left in peace? 
2 CONT Will these intrusions never cease?" 
2 CONT
2 CONT But I was different from before, 
2 CONT My whole intent was altered. 
2 CONT "I've just been reading of the war, 
2 CONT The Russian war," I faltered. 
2 CONT "I am a Finn like you, and oh! 
2 CONT There's so much more I long to know." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Such was my greeting. In surprise 
2 CONT He looked straight up, looked harder, 
2 CONT And of a sudden his old eyes 
2 CONT Flashed with a warrior's ardor. 
2 CONT "Aye, my young master, if you'd hear, 
2 CONT I'll tell you right, for I was there." 
2 CONT
2 CONT I sat me on his couch of straw, 
2 CONT And story followed story. 
2 CONT What stirring battlescenes I saw, 
2 CONT What deeds of deathless glory, 
2 CONT While strange and beauteous light was shed 
2 CONT Round that old soldier's grizzled head! 
2 CONT
2 CONT So many bloody victories 
2 CONT He'd shared, and all so often 
2 CONT Defeat, with wounds of agony 
2 CONT That time could never soften. 
2 CONT Much that the world had else forgot 
2 CONT Still burned within him fierce and hot. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I sat there listening on and on 
2 CONT In silence, breathless, hearted, 
2 CONT And the night was nearly gone 
2 CONT When I at last departed. 
2 CONT He rose and saw me to the door, 
2 CONT Pressing my offered hand once more. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From that time forth my visits came 
2 CONT To be a daily fixture; 
2 CONT Our joys, our sorrows were the same, 
2 CONT We both smoked my good mixture. 
2 CONT He was a sage, and I a fool, 
2 CONT A king he was, and I at school. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These tales I've sought to give in rhyme 
2 CONT Are what the ensign told me; 
2 CONT Beneath his flaring pine-torch, time 
2 CONT And time again they'd hold me. 
2 CONT Here in the simplest words they stand. 
2 CONT Take them, beloved fatherland!
1 NOTE Studerade klassiska språk och filosofi vid Åbo akademi. Han blev docent i latin 1830. Disp i grekiska 1833  Gifte sig med sysslingen Fredrika Tengström. Flyttade till Borgå 1837 som lektor i latin, från 1842 i grekiska. Rekto
2 CONC r 1847-50. Audiens hos Oscar 1 och prins Gustaf 1851.  År 1863 förlamades han av slaganfall. Några av hans verk var: Fänriks Ståls sägner del 1 o 2 år 1848 resp 1860, Nadeschda 1841, Finska nationalsången 1847 o psalmboken
2 CONC  1857
2 CONT
2 CONT J.L. Runeberg diktade på svenska och hans verk översattes till finska och många andra språk. Runeberg tog kandidatexamen och fil.magister 1827. Han blev amanuens och docent i latin 1830. 1837 blev han lektor i romersk litterat
2 CONC ur vid gymnasiet i Borgå. Runeberg prästvigdes 1838, blev lektor i grekiska 1842, fick professorstitel 1844 och blev teologie doktor 1857.
